# Zembil — environment configuration
#
# Copy this file to `.env` in the same directory as docker-compose.yml, then
# edit it:
#
#   cp .env.example .env
#
# `.env` is read automatically by `docker compose`. It is git-ignored — keep
# it that way, since ZEMBIL_BOOTSTRAP_ADMIN_PASSWORD (if you set one
# yourself) is a secret and this file's whole job is to keep secrets out of
# docker-compose.yml and out of the image. See README.md, "Secrets".
#
# Full reference for every variable below: docs/CONTRACT.md §6. This file
# exists so a fresh clone has them all in one place with a default and a
# one-line reason; if the two ever disagree, CONTRACT.md is the one that's
# right.

# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Required
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------

# The externally-visible origin of this deployment: scheme + host, and a port
# only if it isn't 80/443. No trailing slash. Drives the mandatory Origin
# check on every mutating request (CONTRACT.md §3) and WebAuthn's
# expectedOrigin. The app refuses to start if this is unset or fails to
# parse as a URL — change the placeholder below, don't just uncomment it.
ZEMBIL_ORIGIN=https://zembil.example.com

# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# WebAuthn / passkeys
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------

# The WebAuthn relying-party ID. Leave this commented out: it then defaults
# to the FULL HOSTNAME of ZEMBIL_ORIGIN above (e.g. zembil.example.com),
# which is almost always what you want. Only set it explicitly if you have
# read the paragraph below and mean it.
#
# THIS IS A ONE-WAY DOOR (docs/DECISIONS.md D-022). An rpID is a *scope*, not
# a label: a passkey minted for "example.com" can be requested by literally
# any page under *.example.com, including anything else you happen to run on
# a sibling subdomain of this same host. And authenticators key credentials
# by rpID, so changing this value AFTER any family member has registered a
# passkey silently invalidates every single one of them — there is no
# migration and no warning at the moment of loss, just a passkey login that
# quietly stops working the next time someone tries it, on every device.
# Pick the exact hostname once, before the first passkey is ever registered,
# and then never touch this again.
# ZEMBIL_RP_ID=zembil.example.com

# Shown in the OS/browser passkey prompt ("Save a passkey for Zembil on this
# device?"). Cosmetic only.
# ZEMBIL_RP_NAME=Zembil

# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# First-admin bootstrap — read this before the first `docker compose up`
# (CONTRACT.md §3.8, docs/DECISIONS.md D-014)
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# On first start, if the users table is empty, the app creates exactly one
# admin account from the two variables below and marks it
# must-change-password. This runs in-process, before the server starts
# listening — no separate setup step, so one `docker compose up` really is
# enough. It is a no-op on every later restart (it only ever acts when the
# users table is EMPTY), so leaving these two variables set in `.env` after
# the first boot is harmless — it will never reset an existing admin's
# password.
#
# If you leave ZEMBIL_BOOTSTRAP_ADMIN_PASSWORD unset, a random 20-character
# password is generated and written to the container's log exactly ONCE, at
# warn level:
#
#   docker compose logs zembil | grep -A6 'first-admin'
#
# right after the very first `docker compose up` is the only place it will
# ever appear — it is not stored anywhere in plaintext and cannot be
# recovered later. Copy it somewhere safe immediately. See README.md,
# "First-admin bootstrap" and "Recovering a forgotten admin password".

# ZEMBIL_BOOTSTRAP_ADMIN_USERNAME=admin
# ZEMBIL_BOOTSTRAP_ADMIN_PASSWORD=

# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Reverse proxy / networking
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------

# How many trusted hops of X-Forwarded-For to count back from the right-hand
# side, to find the real client IP for rate limiting (CONTRACT.md §3.7). 1 is
# correct for the standard shape this README documents: internet -> your
# reverse proxy -> this container, which adds exactly one hop. Set to 0 only
# if there is genuinely no reverse proxy in front of this container at all —
# not the deployment README.md documents.
# ZEMBIL_TRUST_PROXY=1

# The HOST-side port docker-compose.yml publishes to 127.0.0.1. This is a
# convenience of this compose file only — it is NOT one of the app's own
# environment variables, and inside the container adapter-node always
# listens on PORT=3000 regardless of what you set here. Change it only if
# 3000 is already taken by something else on the host.
# ZEMBIL_HOST_PORT=3000

# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Sessions
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------

# ZEMBIL_SESSION_IDLE_DAYS=30
# ZEMBIL_SESSION_ABSOLUTE_DAYS=180

# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Durability
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------

# NORMAL (default) or FULL, read by src/lib/server/db/index.ts. Under WAL,
# NORMAL cannot corrupt the database; on sudden power loss it can lose the last
# few transactions. A home server has no UPS and losing the last few seconds of
# a shopping list is acceptable. Set FULL if the machine is on a UPS and you
# would rather have the durability than the write throughput.
# ZEMBIL_SYNCHRONOUS=NORMAL

# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Logging
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------

# debug | info | warn | error. Validated at startup and rejected if it is not
# one of those four — but nothing filters on it yet, so today it changes
# nothing you can observe. Setting it high will NOT hide the first-admin
# password banner. Tracked in docs/BACKLOG.md.
# ZEMBIL_LOG_LEVEL=info

# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Set by docker-compose.yml already — listed here only because
# docs/CONTRACT.md §6 lists them as part of the app's environment surface.
# Overriding them in `.env` has no effect: docker-compose.yml sets these
# directly rather than reading them from the shell/`.env` environment, so
# that they always agree with the named volume mount and the published port.
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------

# ZEMBIL_DATA_DIR=/data
# PORT=3000
# HOST=0.0.0.0

# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Never set these (CONTRACT.md §6) — not a default to fill in, an instruction
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# PROTOCOL_HEADER and HOST_HEADER are @sveltejs/adapter-node variables that,
# if set, make the app trust the client-supplied X-Forwarded-Proto /
# X-Forwarded-Host headers to decide what its own origin is. Zembil never
# needs them: the mandatory Origin check and WebAuthn's expectedOrigin /
# expectedRPID all come from the constant ZEMBIL_ORIGIN above, never from a
# request header. docker-compose.yml does not set either, and you should not
# add them here — doing so would let a client that reaches this container
# directly, bypassing the reverse proxy, simply claim to be any origin it
# likes.
#
# PROTOCOL_HEADER=
# HOST_HEADER=
